#version 450
// **One workgroup per solid-shell element**, against `solidshell_forces.comp`'s one
// *invocation* per element. Same element, same arithmetic, same order of
// accumulation; only the mapping onto the machine differs.
//
// --- Why this file exists ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// `07-fem-spike-findings.md` §8 measured the one-thread-per-element kernel at
// 0.25x/0.41x/0.68x/0.24x/0.23x of the 23-thread CPU across 192..16 384 elements --
// improving with occupancy to ~3 000 elements and then getting *worse*, which is the
// shape that identifies register spilling rather than the element. One thread per
// element needs about five hundred floats of thread-private, dynamically indexed
// state: two copies of eight integration points of history, a 6x6 algorithmic
// tangent, a 7x7 Kaa and its Cholesky factor, and four 24-vectors. Pascal has 255
// registers per thread, so all of it spills to local memory -- global memory with an
// L2 in front -- and past ~3 000 elements the spill working set leaves the 2 MB L2.
// §8's own words: "the current numbers measure register spilling, not the element."
//
// So this is the mapping §8 and `06-roadmap.md` both name as the one to try.
//
// --- What each of the 32 threads does ----------------------------------------------
//
// The element's work is not uniform, so neither is the split. Each phase uses the
// widest split it has:
//
//   nodes/DOF phase   24 threads, one per degree of freedom -- loading `rest` and
//                     `current`, forming `u = R^T x - X`, and the final `f = -R f_int`
//   strain phase      up to 48 tasks (8 Gauss points x 6 Voigt components), one
//                     B-row dot product each, strided over the 32 threads
//   return map        **8 threads, one per Gauss point** -- this is the part that
//                     cannot be split further, and it is why a point's plastic
//                     history lives in *its own thread's registers* instead of all
//                     eight copies living in every thread's. That single change is
//                     what takes the private working set from ~500 floats to ~50.
//   Kaa phase         49 tasks (the 7x7 block), strided over the 32 threads. This is
//                     ~70% of the arithmetic in a Newton iteration -- 8 x 49 x 36
//                     multiply-adds against the return map's ~4 000 -- so it is the
//                     one that had to parallelise well.
//   solve phase       thread 0 alone: equilibrate, 7x7 Cholesky, forward/back
//                     substitution. ~200 flops, not worth a reduction.
//
// The 6x6 tangents the Kaa phase consumes are produced by the return-map phase in a
// *different* thread, so they go to shared memory (8 x 36 floats). That is the whole
// trade: 288 floats of shared for eight tangents, against eight tangents' worth of
// spill traffic per thread.
//
// **Every `barrier()` is reached by all 32 threads.** Each loop break tests a value
// that was written to shared memory by one thread and read after a barrier, so it is
// workgroup-uniform by construction -- which is what makes the barriers legal. A
// break on a per-thread condition would deadlock.
//
// --- A hazard that no test on this machine can see ---------------------------------
//
// **On the 1070 Ti the subgroup size is 32 and this workgroup is 32, so a workgroup
// is exactly one subgroup and most of these barriers synchronise threads the
// hardware has already synchronised.** `zone_gpu_probe --stats` reports the
// subgroup size for this reason; it is a measurement rather than an inference from
// the vendor.
//
// Mutation testing says so directly: of four mutants that each delete one
// `barrier()`, **three survive the entire suite**. The fourth -- the one before
// `if (sStop != 0) break`, where thread 0's write to `sStop` and `sDelta` is read
// by all 32 -- dies on the cross-mapping damage comparison. It is worth being
// precise about *why*, because the obvious explanation is wrong: it is not that the
// hardware failed to synchronise, since a single-warp workgroup is already in
// lockstep. It is that `barrier()` is also a **memory** barrier, and without it the
// compiler is free to keep a shared value in a register or hoist the read above the
// write. So the difference between the three that survive and the one that dies is
// what this particular driver's optimiser chose to do, not what the hardware did.
//
// That makes the surviving three *more* alarming rather than less: they are correct
// here only by the compiler's grace, and no test in this repository can see it.
//
// They are kept, and they are not optional: they are what the Vulkan memory model
// requires, and on any device with independent thread scheduling (Volta onward) or
// a workgroup spanning more than one subgroup they become load-bearing immediately.
// What this means in practice is that **their correctness rests on the argument
// above rather than on the suite**, and an edit that removes one will pass every
// test in this repository. That is written here because it is the single most
// likely way this file gets silently broken later.
//
// --- What is deliberately unchanged ------------------------------------------------
//
// Forces still go to a **per-element slot** and the companion node kernel still
// gathers them over the CSR adjacency in a fixed order. That is what makes the result
// reproducible without float atomics, and it is the reason `tet_forces.comp` is shaped
// the way it is. Nothing here touches it: the same 24 floats land in the same slot in
// the same order, so the two mappings are interchangeable behind an identical buffer.
//
// Everything else -- the return map, the damage law, the enhanced-strain Newton, the
// tolerances, the two-attempt degradation rule -- is `solidshell_forces.comp` term for
// term, so the two kernels can be diffed against each other and against the CPU.
//
// --- The enhanced block in double, and what that can and cannot buy -----------------
//
// `SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64` compiles this one source into several kernels, differing only in
// how much of the 7x7 enhanced-strain block runs in fp64. It exists because
// `07-fem-spike-findings.md` §8's last open item was "keep alpha in double -- the only
// part that has been shown to need the digits", and the three things that claim bundles
// together are separable:
//
//   0  everything float, as shipped.
//   1  **the linear solve**: the Jacobi equilibration, the 7x7 Cholesky and its two
//      triangular substitutions, on thread 0. Kaa and the residual still arrive as
//      float, so this only asks whether the *factorisation* was losing the digits.
//   2  **+ the condensation**: Kaa = sum w G^T C G and r = sum w G^T sigma accumulated
//      in double, reading G and the Gauss weights from a **double** copy of the rest
//      forms. The double copy is not optional at this level: a double accumulation
//      over a float G is still an operator known to seven digits, so without it the
//      level would measure nothing.
//   3  **+ the Newton loop**: alpha itself, its correction, its contribution to the
//      strain, its per-element persistent storage (a separate fp64 buffer, so it is
//      not rounded to float at every step boundary), and the CPU's own stopping rule.
//
// **What no level of this can reach, and it is worth writing down before any of it is
// measured.** Kaa's inputs are the algorithmic tangent C and the stress sigma, both
// produced by a float return map into float shared memory. Widening the arithmetic that
// consumes them cannot recover digits they never had, so the enhanced block's ceiling
// is float's ~1e-7 on C and sigma however this is compiled. That ceiling was not
// binding when §8 wrote its item 3, because kappa(Kaa) was then (h/t)^4 ~ 1e7 and the
// block genuinely had no correct digits; `computeRestForms`'s normalisation has since
// put kappa(Kaa) at a constant 3.50. A well-conditioned 7x7 solve on float inputs
// already delivers most of what float can deliver.
//
// That is an argument, and this file exists so it can be a measurement instead.
#ifndef SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64
#define SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 0
#endif

// The Newton's stopping rule, **separately selectable from the arithmetic**. The
// shipped float kernel converges on `|delta . r| <= 1e-9 * sigma_y V` in at most 12
// iterations where the CPU uses `1e-16` and 40, because 1e-16 of a float quantity is
// below the noise the same float quantity carries and the loop would simply run to its
// cap every step. Level 3 adopts the CPU's rule, which means it changes two things at
// once -- so `SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT=1` on its own builds a **float** kernel with the tight
// rule, and that is the control that tells the tolerance and the arithmetic apart.
#ifndef SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT
#define SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT (SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3)
#endif

// `easreal` is the type of the 7x7 solve's own state -- Kaa, its Cholesky factor, the
// equilibration's D, the residual and the correction. `condreal` is the type the
// condensation *accumulates* in, which is deliberately not the same knob: at level 1
// Kaa is stored in double but each entry is still summed in float, so the level really
// is "only the linear solve". `alphareal` is alpha's own type.
//
// At level 0 all three expand to `float` and the preprocessed text is character for
// character what it was before this knob existed -- checked, not assumed, by comparing
// the level-0 SPIR-V against the shipped kernel's.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 1
#define easreal double
#else
#define easreal float
#endif
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
#define condreal double
#else
#define condreal float
#endif
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3
#define alphareal double
#else
#define alphareal float
#endif

layout(local_size_x = 32) in;

// std430, all float unless stated. Layouts match the host in zone_gpu.cpp, and are
// **identical** to solidshell_forces.comp's -- the host uploads one set of buffers
// and either kernel reads them.
layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly  buffer Positions  { float position[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 4) readonly  buffer Indices    { uint  index[]; };      // 8 per element
layout(std430, binding = 5) readonly  buffer Forms      { float forms[]; };      // kFormStride each
layout(std430, binding = 6) readonly  buffer RestPos    { float restPosition[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 7) writeonly buffer ElemForce  { float elementForce[]; };  // 24 each
layout(std430, binding = 10)          buffer PlasticSt  { float plastic[]; };    // kStateStride each
layout(std430, binding = 11)          buffer ElemOut    { float elementOut[]; }; // 1 each
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
// The enhanced-strain operator in double: G then the Gauss weights, kEasFormStride per
// element. A second copy rather than a widened `forms`, because B is 1 152 of the
// 1 505 floats and nothing in the enhanced block reads it -- widening the whole thing
// would triple the residency of the largest buffer to serve a fifth of it. The host
// allocates it only when a kernel that declares it is the one being loaded.
layout(std430, binding = 14) readonly buffer EasForms   { double easForms[]; };
#endif
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3
// alpha's persistent per-element state, kEas doubles each. The float copy in
// `plastic[kStateEnhanced..]` is still written, because that is what `readback` and
// every test read; this is what the *next step* warm-starts from, which is the half
// that a float round trip would quietly undo.
layout(std430, binding = 15)          buffer Enhanced64 { double easAlpha[]; };
#endif

layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
    float dt;
    float mu;             // shear modulus
    float kappa;          // bulk modulus
    float yieldStrength;  // sigma_y(0)
    float strengthCoefficient;   // Swift K
    float referenceStrain;       // Swift eps_0
    float hardeningExponent;     // Swift n
    float kinematicModulus;
    float triaxialitySensitivity;
    float referenceTriaxiality;
    float cutoffTriaxiality;
    float punchSpeed;
    float axisX, axisY, axisZ;
    uint  nodeCount;
    uint  elementCount;
    uint  drivenCount;
} params;

const int  kNodes = 8;
const int  kDof = 24;
const int  kEas = 7;
const int  kGauss = 8;
// b[8][6][24] + g[8][6][7] + weight[8] + restJacobianInverse[9]
const uint kFormB = 0u;
const uint kFormG = 1152u;
const uint kFormW = 1488u;
const uint kFormJ = 1496u;
const uint kFormStride = 1505u;
// per Gauss point: plasticStrain[6], backStress[6], eps_p, damage, failed
const uint kPointStride = 15u;
// 8 points, then enhanced[7], failureStrain, torn
const uint kStateEnhanced = 120u;
const uint kStateFailure = 127u;
const uint kStateTorn = 128u;
const uint kStateStride = 129u;
// The fp64 enhanced-strain forms, when they exist: g[8][6][7] then weight[8]. Inside
// the guard so that at level 0 this file compiles to **byte-identical** SPIR-V to the
// kernel it replaces -- two unused constants are two extra ids, and a comparison that
// has to be qualified is worth less than one that does not.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
const uint kEasFormG = 0u;
const uint kEasFormW = 336u;
const uint kEasFormStride = 344u;
#endif

const float kRoot23 = 0.816496580927726f;

// Identical to solidshell_forces.comp's. **The reason recorded for the 1e-9 -- that the
// CPU's 1e-16 is "below what float can represent at all" -- has been measured and is
// wrong**, and the correction matters because the tolerance turns out to be the only
// thing on this whole file that changes the answer.
//
// What the 1e-9 gate actually does is fire on the *first* iteration of a lightly loaded
// element, and the test sits after the correction is computed and before it is applied
// -- so alpha never leaves its warm start and the enhanced modes are simply off. On the
// 192-element ferry patch at 50 steps the device returns bit-zero for all seven
// parameters of every element where the CPU returns 7.7e-9, and `--eas=solve` and
// `--eas=condense` return the identical zero: they compute the same correction in fp64
// and discard it at the same gate. Float at the CPU's 1e-16 does *not* stall; it runs
// more iterations and recovers alpha to about 20%.
//
// It is left at 1e-9 because the CPU's rule costs several times as much on this kernel and
// does not fix what the kernel is actually wrong about -- see `07-fem-spike-findings.md`
// §8, "Alpha in double", which carries the cost table. The multiple was written here as
// 3.5x and has since been re-measured higher; a wall clock does not belong in a comment
// that nothing re-runs, and on a shared box it cannot be re-measured honestly anyway.
// What *is* stable and worth keeping here: the tight rule tears 40 at 768 against float's
// 40 and the CPU's 32, and 205 at 3 072 against 247 and 162 -- it moves the answer at the
// larger size and not at the smaller, which is the shape of the argument.
// `SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT` builds the variant that uses it. The residual gate is
// *already* the CPU's 1e-12, so only the work gate and the iteration cap move.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT
const easreal kEnhancedWorkTol = easreal(1.0e-16);
#else
const easreal kEnhancedWorkTol = easreal(1.0e-9);
#endif
const float kResidualTol = 1.0e-12f;
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_TIGHT
const int   kMaxIterations = 40;
#else
const int   kMaxIterations = 12;
#endif

// --- Shared: the per-element state that used to be thread-private -----------------
//
// ~500 floats, once per element, instead of ~500 floats once per *thread*.
shared float sRest[kDof];
shared float sCurrent[kDof];
shared float sU[kDof];
shared float sR[9];               // rotation, row-major, matching the CPU's `r`
shared float sStrain[kGauss * 6];
shared float sStress[kGauss * 6];
shared float sTangent[kGauss * 36];   // produced per point, consumed by the Kaa phase
shared easreal sKaa[kEas * kEas];
shared easreal sChol[kEas * kEas];
shared easreal sResidual[kEas];
shared alphareal sAlpha[kEas];
shared easreal sDelta[kEas];
shared easreal sScaleD[kEas];     // the Jacobi equilibration's D
shared float sDissipation[kGauss];
shared int   sPointFailed[kGauss];
shared float sYieldEnergy;        // sigma_y * V, the scale both tolerances are on
shared float sFailureStrain;
shared int   sEasCount;
shared int   sStop;               // set by thread 0; every loop break reads this
shared int   sDegraded;

// ||dev sigma||^2 with the engineering-shear convention: entries 3..5 are tensor
// components here (this is a stress), so they count twice. Dropping that 2 gives a
// yield surface that is no longer isotropic while still passing every uniaxial test.
float tensorNormSquared(float v[6]) {
    return v[0] * v[0] + v[1] * v[1] + v[2] * v[2] +
           2.0f * (v[3] * v[3] + v[4] * v[4] + v[5] * v[5]);
}

float flowStress(float p) {
    p = max(0.0f, p);
    return params.strengthCoefficient * pow(params.referenceStrain + p, params.hardeningExponent);
}
float flowSlope(float p) {
    p = max(0.0f, p);
    return params.strengthCoefficient * params.hardeningExponent *
           pow(params.referenceStrain + p, params.hardeningExponent - 1.0f);
}

// Rotation part of F by Higham's Newton iteration, as tet_forces.comp.
mat3 polarRotation(mat3 f) {
    mat3 r = f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) r = 0.5 * (r + transpose(inverse(r)));
    return r;
}

// --- One Gauss point's plastic state, held by one thread --------------------------
//
// This is the whole point of the remap. In the one-thread-per-element kernel every
// thread carried all eight of these twice over; here thread `gp` carries exactly its
// own, in registers, statically indexed.
struct Point {
    float plasticStrain[6];
    float backStress[6];
    float equivalentPlasticStrain;
    float damage;
    bool  failed;
};

Point gStart;   // state at the top of the step -- the Newton restarts from it
Point gTrial;   // state after the current trial strain

// sim::plasticity::update, term for term with solidshell_forces.comp's `returnMap`.
// Writes this point's stress, tangent and dissipation to shared for the Kaa phase.
void returnMap(int gp, float strain[6]) {
    gTrial = gStart;
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) sStress[gp * 6 + i] = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 36; ++i) sTangent[gp * 36 + i] = 0.0f;
    sDissipation[gp] = 0.0f;
    if (gTrial.failed) { sPointFailed[gp] = 1; return; }

    const float mu = params.mu, kappa = params.kappa;

    float elastic[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) elastic[i] = strain[i] - gTrial.plasticStrain[i];
    const float volumetric = elastic[0] + elastic[1] + elastic[2];
    const float pressure = kappa * volumetric;

    float trial[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        trial[i] = 2.0f * mu * (elastic[i] - volumetric / 3.0f);
        trial[3 + i] = mu * elastic[3 + i];
    }
    float relative[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) relative[i] = trial[i] - gTrial.backStress[i];
    const float relativeNorm = sqrt(tensorNormSquared(relative));

    const float yieldRadius = kRoot23 * flowStress(gTrial.equivalentPlasticStrain);
    const float excess = relativeNorm - yieldRadius;

    if (!(excess > 0.0f)) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
            sStress[gp * 6 + i] = pressure + trial[i];
            sStress[gp * 6 + 3 + i] = trial[3 + i];
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
                sTangent[gp * 36 + i * 6 + j] =
                    kappa + 2.0f * mu * ((i == j ? 1.0f : 0.0f) - 1.0f / 3.0f);
            sTangent[gp * 36 + (3 + i) * 6 + (3 + i)] = mu;
        }
        sPointFailed[gp] = 0;
        return;
    }

    // Consistency. g is decreasing, g(0) = excess > 0, and the no-hardening bound
    // brackets the root -- so the loop is entered from above and bisection is only
    // the fallback. Same structure as the CPU, with a float tolerance.
    const float linearPart = 2.0f * mu + (2.0f / 3.0f) * params.kinematicModulus;
    float lo = 0.0f, hi = excess / linearPart;
    float gamma = hi;
    for (int it = 0; it < 30; ++it) {
        const float accumulated = gTrial.equivalentPlasticStrain + kRoot23 * gamma;
        const float residual = relativeNorm - linearPart * gamma - kRoot23 * flowStress(accumulated);
        if (residual > 0.0f) lo = gamma; else hi = gamma;
        if (abs(residual) <= 1.0e-7f * yieldRadius) break;
        const float slope = -linearPart - (2.0f / 3.0f) * flowSlope(accumulated);
        float next = gamma - residual / slope;
        if (!(next > lo && next < hi)) next = 0.5f * (lo + hi);
        if (abs(next - gamma) <= 1.0e-7f * gamma) { gamma = next; break; }
        gamma = next;
    }

    float direction[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) direction[i] = relative[i] / relativeNorm;

    float plasticIncrement[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        plasticIncrement[i] = gamma * direction[i];
        plasticIncrement[3 + i] = 2.0f * gamma * direction[3 + i];
    }
    float st[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        st[i] = pressure + trial[i] - 2.0f * mu * gamma * direction[i];
        st[3 + i] = trial[3 + i] - 2.0f * mu * gamma * direction[3 + i];
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) sStress[gp * 6 + i] = st[i];

    float back[6], effective[6];
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
        back[i] = gTrial.backStress[i] + (2.0f / 3.0f) * params.kinematicModulus * gamma * direction[i];
        effective[i] = st[i] - back[i];
    }

    float dissipation = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) dissipation += effective[i] * plasticIncrement[i];
    sDissipation[gp] = dissipation;

    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
        gTrial.plasticStrain[i] += plasticIncrement[i];
        gTrial.backStress[i] = back[i];
    }
    const float dEps = kRoot23 * gamma;
    gTrial.equivalentPlasticStrain += dEps;

    // Damage, off the returned stress's own invariants.
    float deviator[6];
    const float mean = (st[0] + st[1] + st[2]) / 3.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) { deviator[i] = st[i] - mean; deviator[3 + i] = st[3 + i]; }
    const float q = sqrt(1.5f * tensorNormSquared(deviator));
    const float eta = q > 0.0f ? mean / q : 0.0f;
    // Below the cutoff voids close rather than grow and the critical strain is
    // infinite, so no damage accumulates at all.
    if (eta > params.cutoffTriaxiality) {
        const float critical =
            sFailureStrain * exp(-params.triaxialitySensitivity * (eta - params.referenceTriaxiality));
        if (critical > 0.0f) gTrial.damage += dEps / critical;
    }

    if (gTrial.damage >= 1.0f) {
        gTrial.damage = 1.0f;
        gTrial.failed = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) sStress[gp * 6 + i] = 0.0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < 36; ++i) sTangent[gp * 36 + i] = 0.0f;
        sPointFailed[gp] = 1;
        return;
    }
    sPointFailed[gp] = 0;

    const float theta = 1.0f - 2.0f * mu * gamma / relativeNorm;
    const float plasticModulus = flowSlope(gTrial.equivalentPlasticStrain) + params.kinematicModulus;
    const float thetaBar = 1.0f / (1.0f + plasticModulus / (3.0f * mu)) - (1.0f - theta);
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
            sTangent[gp * 36 + i * 6 + j] =
                kappa + 2.0f * mu * theta * ((i == j ? 1.0f : 0.0f) - 1.0f / 3.0f);
        sTangent[gp * 36 + (3 + i) * 6 + (3 + i)] = mu * theta;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j < 6; ++j)
            sTangent[gp * 36 + i * 6 + j] -= 2.0f * mu * thetaBar * direction[i] * direction[j];
}

void main() {
    const uint e = gl_WorkGroupID.x;
    const uint t = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    if (e >= params.elementCount) return;   // uniform over the workgroup

    const uint fb = e * kFormStride;
    const uint sb = e * kStateStride;
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
    const uint eb = e * kEasFormStride;
#endif

    // Already torn: output-identical to running the update, since every point returns
    // no stress and no dissipation. Uniform over the workgroup, so every thread takes
    // the same branch and no barrier is skipped by only some of them.
    if (plastic[sb + kStateTorn] != 0.0f) {
        for (uint i = t; i < uint(kDof); i += gl_WorkGroupSize.x)
            elementForce[e * uint(kDof) + i] = 0.0f;
        return;
    }

    // --- Load, and the co-rotational frame --------------------------------------
    for (uint i = t; i < uint(kDof); i += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
        const uint a = i / 3u, k = i % 3u;
        const uint n = index[e * uint(kNodes) + a];
        sRest[i] = restPosition[n * 3u + k];
        sCurrent[i] = position[n * 3u + k];
    }
    if (t == 0u) {
        sFailureStrain = plastic[sb + kStateFailure];
        float volume = 0.0f;
        for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) volume += forms[fb + kFormW + uint(gp)];
        sYieldEnergy = params.yieldStrength * volume;
        sStop = 0;
    }
    barrier();

    if (t == 0u) {
        // dN_a/dxi at the element centre is 0.125 times the node's own natural
        // coordinate, so the current Jacobian at the centre is one 3x8x3 contraction
        // and needs no shape-function evaluation.
        const float nx[kNodes] = float[](-1., 1., 1., -1., -1., 1., 1., -1.);
        const float ny[kNodes] = float[](-1., -1., 1., 1., -1., -1., 1., 1.);
        const float nz[kNodes] = float[](-1., -1., -1., -1., 1., 1., 1., 1.);
        float jc[3][3];
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) {
                float s = 0.0f;
                for (int a = 0; a < kNodes; ++a) {
                    const float dN = 0.125f * (k == 0 ? nx[a] : (k == 1 ? ny[a] : nz[a]));
                    s += dN * sCurrent[a * 3 + i];
                }
                jc[i][k] = s;
            }
        mat3 f;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j) {
                float s = 0.0f;
                for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) s += jc[i][k] * forms[fb + kFormJ + uint(k * 3 + j)];
                f[j][i] = s;  // GLSL mat3 is column-major: f[col][row]
            }
        const mat3 rot = polarRotation(f);
        // sR[i * 3 + k] is row i, column k, matching the CPU's row-major `r`.
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) sR[i * 3 + k] = rot[k][i];
    }
    barrier();

    // u = R^T x - X, one degree of freedom per thread.
    for (uint d = t; d < uint(kDof); d += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
        const uint a = d / 3u, i = d % 3u;
        float s = 0.0f;
        for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) s += sR[i * 3u + uint(k)] * sCurrent[a * 3u + uint(k)];
        sU[d] = s - sRest[d];
    }

    // --- The enhanced strains ------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Thread `gp` owns Gauss point `gp`'s history, in its own registers.
    if (t < uint(kGauss)) {
        const uint p = sb + t * kPointStride;
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
            gStart.plasticStrain[i] = plastic[p + uint(i)];
            gStart.backStress[i] = plastic[p + 6u + uint(i)];
        }
        gStart.equivalentPlasticStrain = plastic[p + 12u];
        gStart.damage = plastic[p + 13u];
        gStart.failed = plastic[p + 14u] != 0.0f;
        sPointFailed[t] = gStart.failed ? 1 : 0;
    }
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3
    if (t < uint(kEas)) sAlpha[t] = easAlpha[e * uint(kEas) + t];
#else
    if (t < uint(kEas)) sAlpha[t] = plastic[sb + kStateEnhanced + t];
#endif
    barrier();

    // An element that has lost a point drops its enhanced modes and finishes its life
    // as the ANS hex: r(alpha) = 0 is a statement about a continuum and an element
    // with a dead point is not one. Same rule, same reason, as the CPU.
    if (t == 0u) {
        int degraded = 0;
        for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) degraded += sPointFailed[gp];
        sEasCount = degraded > 0 ? 0 : kEas;
    }
    barrier();
    if (sEasCount == 0 && t < uint(kEas)) sAlpha[t] = alphareal(0.0);
    barrier();

    // Two attempts at most: the second is entered only when a point died inside this
    // step, which is precisely the step in which Kaa was losing rank while the Newton
    // was solving on it.
    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 2; ++attempt) {
        if (t == 0u) sStop = 0;
        barrier();

        for (int iteration = 0; iteration < kMaxIterations; ++iteration) {
            // Strain: 8 Gauss points x 6 Voigt components, one B-row dot product each.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3
            // The enhanced part summed in double against the double G, then added to
            // the float B u and rounded **once** into the strain the return map takes.
            // `sStrain` stays float on purpose: it is the enhanced block's interface to
            // the rest of the element, and widening it would be widening the element.
            for (uint task = t; task < uint(kGauss * 6); task += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
                const uint gp = task / 6u, i = task % 6u;
                const uint bb = fb + kFormB + gp * 144u;
                const uint gb = eb + kEasFormG + gp * 42u;
                float s = 0.0f;
                for (int j = 0; j < kDof; ++j) s += forms[bb + i * uint(kDof) + uint(j)] * sU[j];
                double enhanced = 0.0;
                for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k)
                    enhanced += easForms[gb + i * uint(kEas) + uint(k)] * sAlpha[k];
                sStrain[task] = float(double(s) + enhanced);
            }
#else
            for (uint task = t; task < uint(kGauss * 6); task += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
                const uint gp = task / 6u, i = task % 6u;
                const uint bb = fb + kFormB + gp * 144u;
                const uint gb = fb + kFormG + gp * 42u;
                float s = 0.0f;
                for (int j = 0; j < kDof; ++j) s += forms[bb + i * uint(kDof) + uint(j)] * sU[j];
                for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k)
                    s += forms[gb + i * uint(kEas) + uint(k)] * sAlpha[k];
                sStrain[task] = s;
            }
#endif
            barrier();

            if (t < uint(kGauss)) {
                float strain[6];
                for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) strain[i] = sStrain[t * 6u + uint(i)];
                returnMap(int(t), strain);
            }
            barrier();

            if (sEasCount == 0) break;   // uniform: sEasCount is shared and set by t == 0

            // Residual r_k = sum_gp w_gp * sum_i G[gp][i][k] * sigma[gp][i].
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
            if (t < uint(kEas)) {
                double s = 0.0;
                for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
                    const uint gb = eb + kEasFormG + uint(gp) * 42u;
                    const double w = easForms[eb + kEasFormW + uint(gp)];
                    double inner = 0.0;
                    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
                        inner += easForms[gb + uint(i) * uint(kEas) + t] * sStress[gp * 6 + i];
                    s += w * inner;
                }
                sResidual[t] = s;
            }
#else
            if (t < uint(kEas)) {
                float s = 0.0f;
                for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
                    const uint gb = fb + kFormG + uint(gp) * 42u;
                    const float w = forms[fb + kFormW + uint(gp)];
                    float inner = 0.0f;
                    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
                        inner += forms[gb + uint(i) * uint(kEas) + t] * sStress[gp * 6 + i];
                    s += w * inner;
                }
                sResidual[t] = s;
            }
#endif
            barrier();

            if (t == 0u) {
                easreal norm = easreal(0.0);
                for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k) norm += sResidual[k] * sResidual[k];
                sStop = sqrt(norm) <= kResidualTol * sYieldEnergy ? 1 : 0;
            }
            barrier();
            if (sStop != 0) break;

            // Kaa = sum_gp w_gp * G^T C G, its 49 entries strided over the 32 threads.
            // ~70% of a Newton iteration's arithmetic, and the reason this phase is the
            // one that had to spread wide.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 2
            for (uint entry = t; entry < uint(kEas * kEas); entry += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
                const uint p = entry / uint(kEas), qq = entry % uint(kEas);
                if (int(p) >= sEasCount || int(qq) >= sEasCount) { sKaa[entry] = 0.0; continue; }
                double acc = 0.0;
                for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
                    const uint gb = eb + kEasFormG + uint(gp) * 42u;
                    const double w = easForms[eb + kEasFormW + uint(gp)];
                    double s = 0.0;
                    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
                        double cg = 0.0;
                        for (int m = 0; m < 6; ++m)
                            cg += sTangent[gp * 36 + i * 6 + m] * easForms[gb + uint(m) * uint(kEas) + qq];
                        s += easForms[gb + uint(i) * uint(kEas) + p] * cg;
                    }
                    acc += w * s;
                }
                sKaa[entry] = acc;
            }
#else
            for (uint entry = t; entry < uint(kEas * kEas); entry += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
                const uint p = entry / uint(kEas), qq = entry % uint(kEas);
                if (int(p) >= sEasCount || int(qq) >= sEasCount) { sKaa[entry] = 0.0f; continue; }
                float acc = 0.0f;
                for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
                    const uint gb = fb + kFormG + uint(gp) * 42u;
                    const float w = forms[fb + kFormW + uint(gp)];
                    float s = 0.0f;
                    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
                        float cg = 0.0f;
                        for (int m = 0; m < 6; ++m)
                            cg += sTangent[gp * 36 + i * 6 + m] * forms[gb + uint(m) * uint(kEas) + qq];
                        s += forms[gb + uint(i) * uint(kEas) + p] * cg;
                    }
                    acc += w * s;
                }
                sKaa[entry] = acc;
            }
#endif
            barrier();

            // The 7x7 solve: thread 0 alone. ~200 flops against the Kaa phase's 14 000,
            // so a reduction would cost more than it saves.
            if (t == 0u) {
                // **Kaa is equilibrated before it is factored -- and this is now
                // redundant.** `computeRestForms` normalises the enhanced modes, so
                // kappa(Kaa) is a constant 3.50 before this shader sees G and there
                // is nothing for a diagonal scaling to recover. Mutation testing
                // confirms it: replacing the scaling with 1.0 survives the whole
                // suite. Kept for parity with solidshell_forces.comp and as the only
                // guard against a `RestForms` built without the normalisation.
                // Jacobi equilibration --
                // solve for D alpha with D = sqrt(diag Kaa) -- is exact in exact
                // arithmetic, so it changes nothing the CPU would compute, and by van
                // der Sluis's theorem it leaves the condition number within a factor of
                // seven of the best any diagonal scaling could give. Kept identical to
                // solidshell_forces.comp so the two mappings can be diffed; whether it
                // still earns its place now that `computeRestForms` normalises the
                // enhanced modes is a measurement, not an assumption, and
                // `07-fem-spike-findings.md` §8 records it.
                bool ok = true;
                for (int i = 0; i < sEasCount; ++i) {
                    const easreal diagonal = sKaa[i * kEas + i];
                    if (!(diagonal > easreal(0.0))) { ok = false; break; }
                    sScaleD[i] = easreal(1.0) / sqrt(diagonal);
                }
                if (ok) {
                    for (int i = 0; i < sEasCount; ++i)
                        for (int j = 0; j < sEasCount; ++j)
                            sKaa[i * kEas + j] *= sScaleD[i] * sScaleD[j];

                    // Cholesky on the equilibrated Kaa, mirroring solveSmall. A
                    // non-positive pivot means the element has no stiffness left, which
                    // is a state to leave rather than to iterate on.
                    for (int k = 0; k < kEas * kEas; ++k) sChol[k] = easreal(0.0);
                    for (int i = 0; i < sEasCount && ok; ++i)
                        for (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {
                            easreal s = sKaa[i * kEas + j];
                            for (int k = 0; k < j; ++k) s -= sChol[i * kEas + k] * sChol[j * kEas + k];
                            if (i == j) {
                                if (!(s > easreal(0.0))) { ok = false; break; }
                                sChol[i * kEas + i] = sqrt(s);
                            } else {
                                sChol[i * kEas + j] = s / sChol[j * kEas + j];
                            }
                        }
                }
                if (!ok) {
                    sStop = 1;
                } else {
                    easreal delta[kEas];
                    for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k) delta[k] = -sResidual[k] * sScaleD[k];
                    for (int i = 0; i < sEasCount; ++i) {
                        easreal s = delta[i];
                        for (int k = 0; k < i; ++k) s -= sChol[i * kEas + k] * delta[k];
                        delta[i] = s / sChol[i * kEas + i];
                    }
                    for (int i = sEasCount - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
                        easreal s = delta[i];
                        for (int k = i + 1; k < sEasCount; ++k) s -= sChol[k * kEas + i] * delta[k];
                        delta[i] = s / sChol[i * kEas + i];
                    }
                    for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k) delta[k] *= sScaleD[k];
                    for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k) sDelta[k] = delta[k];

                    // Converge on the correction's energy, not on ||r||: delta . r is
                    // joules and sigma_y * V is the element's yield energy, so the ratio
                    // is dimensionless however the modes are normalised.
                    easreal work = easreal(0.0);
                    for (int k = 0; k < sEasCount; ++k) work += delta[k] * sResidual[k];
                    sStop = abs(work) <= kEnhancedWorkTol * sYieldEnergy ? 1 : 0;
                }
            }
            barrier();
            if (sStop != 0) break;

            if (t < uint(sEasCount)) sAlpha[t] += alphareal(sDelta[t]);
            barrier();
        }

        // Did a point die inside this step? Uniform, via shared.
        if (t == 0u) {
            int died = 0;
            for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) died += sPointFailed[gp];
            sDegraded = died;
        }
        barrier();
        if (!(sDegraded > 0 && sEasCount > 0)) break;
        if (t == 0u) sEasCount = 0;
        if (t < uint(kEas)) sAlpha[t] = alphareal(0.0);
        barrier();
    }

    // --- Commit --------------------------------------------------------------------

    if (t < uint(kGauss)) {
        const uint p = sb + t * kPointStride;
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
            plastic[p + uint(i)] = gTrial.plasticStrain[i];
            plastic[p + 6u + uint(i)] = gTrial.backStress[i];
        }
        plastic[p + 12u] = gTrial.equivalentPlasticStrain;
        plastic[p + 13u] = gTrial.damage;
        plastic[p + 14u] = gTrial.failed ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
    }
    // All seven, not just sEasCount of them: a degraded element must report zeros
    // rather than the values it had before it started tearing.
#if SHIPSIM_EAS_FP64 >= 3
    // Both copies. `easAlpha` is what the next step warm-starts from and is the one
    // that has to stay in double; the float copy is what `readback` and every test
    // read, and a degraded element has to read zero in *both* or the rule is only
    // half applied.
    if (t < uint(kEas)) {
        easAlpha[e * uint(kEas) + t] = sAlpha[t];
        plastic[sb + kStateEnhanced + t] = float(sAlpha[t]);
    }
#else
    if (t < uint(kEas)) plastic[sb + kStateEnhanced + t] = sAlpha[t];
#endif
    barrier();

    if (t == 0u) {
        float dissipation = 0.0f;
        int failed = 0;
        // Summed in Gauss-point order on one thread, which is the same fixed order the
        // one-invocation kernel and the CPU use. A cross-lane reduction would have been
        // a different order and the two kernels would stop being comparable.
        for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
            dissipation += forms[fb + kFormW + uint(gp)] * sDissipation[gp];
            failed += sPointFailed[gp];
        }
        const bool torn = failed == kGauss;
        plastic[sb + kStateTorn] = torn ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
        elementOut[e] += dissipation;
    }

    // Internal force, one degree of freedom per thread, then rotated back.
    // f_j = sum_gp w_gp * sum_i B[gp][i][j] * sigma[gp][i], accumulated over Gauss
    // points in ascending order -- the same order as the other kernel and the CPU.
    for (uint j = t; j < uint(kDof); j += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
        float acc = 0.0f;
        for (int gp = 0; gp < kGauss; ++gp) {
            const uint bb = fb + kFormB + uint(gp) * 144u;
            const float w = forms[fb + kFormW + uint(gp)];
            float s = 0.0f;
            for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) s += forms[bb + uint(i) * uint(kDof) + j] * sStress[gp * 6 + i];
            acc += w * s;
        }
        sU[j] = acc;   // sU is dead here and the internal force is the same shape
    }
    barrier();

    for (uint d = t; d < uint(kDof); d += gl_WorkGroupSize.x) {
        const uint a = d / 3u, i = d % 3u;
        float s = 0.0f;
        for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) s += sR[uint(k) * 3u + i] * sU[a * 3u + uint(k)];
        elementForce[e * uint(kDof) + d] = -s;
    }
}
